
export anchor DraftingADraftCardCreatesItsStoriesAnd2:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        When "Draft with agent" is run on a card in the Draft column, the stories
        it proposes are created as soon as its run ends, with nothing to accept.

        Once this is done:
        - Each story the draft proposed is added at the end of the Backlog,
          marked "From agent", as accepting it would add it per
          spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.draftingWithAgent. That is the
          first piece with its Spec follow-up step, then each other piece, in the
          order given.
        - The Draft card stays in Draft and is marked "Drafted" in its property
          row, where "3 proposals" shows today.
        - A proposal that fails the checks in
          spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.specGrounding is not created. It
          stays on the card as today, in the warning colour, with "Draft again".
        - A run that proposed nothing, or that failed, leaves the card unmarked,
          as spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.states says.
        - Drafting a "Drafted" card again drafts it again as before.
        - Stories drafted in the Backlog or Next sprint, and drafts started with
          "Ask agent", still wait to be accepted.

        This is an exception to the rule in
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.proposals that nothing reaches the
        plan until it is accepted, so say so there. The spec has nothing on a
        "Drafted" mark yet, so that part is new. Add it to
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.draftingWithAgent and
        spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.states, then change the code to
        match.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: true

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            When "Draft with agent" is run on a card in the Draft column, the stories
            it proposes are created as soon as its run ends, with nothing to accept.

            Once this is done:
            - Each story the draft proposed is added at the end of the Backlog,
              marked "From agent", as accepting it would add it per
              spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.draftingWithAgent. That is the
              first piece with its Spec follow-up step, then each other piece, in the
              order given.
            - The Draft card stays in Draft and is marked "Drafted" in its property
              row, where "3 proposals" shows today.
            - A proposal that fails the checks in
              spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.specGrounding is not created. It
              stays on the card as today, in the warning colour, with "Draft again".
            - A run that proposed nothing, or that failed, leaves the card unmarked,
              as spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.states says.
            - Drafting a "Drafted" card again drafts it again as before.
            - Stories drafted in the Backlog or Next sprint, and drafts started with
              "Ask agent", still wait to be accepted.

            This is an exception to the rule in
            spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.proposals that nothing reaches the
            plan until it is accepted, so say so there. The spec has nothing on a
            "Drafted" mark yet, so that part is new. Add it to
            spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.draftingWithAgent and
            spec/scope/planning/index.pi#Planning.states, then change the code to
            match.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            I've changed the spec only, in `spec/scope/planning/index.pi`. Running "Draft with agent" on a card in Draft now creates the stories it proposes as soon as the run ends, with nothing to accept. `piton check` reports no problems, `piton build` wrote 90 files, and I checked the result in `.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md`.

            What the code should follow:

            - **`Planning.draftingWithAgent`**, new paragraph starting "A story in Draft, drafted with "Draft with agent"…":
              - Each proposed story is created as accepting it would: at the end of the Backlog, marked "From agent". The first piece comes first, with its Spec follow-up step as its own step, then each other piece in the run's order.
              - A proposal that fails the spec grounding checks is not created. It stays on the card as a waiting proposal, in the warning colour, with "Draft again".
              - The card stays in Draft, unchanged. It is marked "Drafted" (muted) in its property row only if at least one story was created. Any proposals left waiting are counted after it, as "Drafted · 1 proposal".
              - The mark is saved in the story's file, survives a reload, and stays if the story is later moved.
              - A run that proposed nothing, had nothing pass the checks, or failed adds no mark, and a mark from an earlier draft stays as it was.
              - Drafting a "Drafted" card again works as before; the new stories are created after the earlier ones.
              - A new paragraph after it says that drafts of Backlog or Next sprint cards, and any "Ask agent" draft (Draft cards included), still wait to be accepted.
              - The "When a run ends…" paragraph now sends Draft stories to this new rule.
            - **`Planning.proposals`**: the first paragraph now names this as the one exception to "nothing reaches the plan until the user accepts it". The application creates the stories, not the agent.
            - **`Planning.states`**: the "A story drafting with the agent" item adds the "Drafted" state and says a failed run leaves the card unmarked.

            I decided three things the prompt didn't settle:

            - **Stopped runs:** a run stopped with "Stop" counts as ended, so it creates whatever it had proposed by then.
            - **Moved cards:** whether a card is in Draft is checked when the run ends, not when it starts. A card moved out of Draft mid-run waits for acceptance; one moved into Draft has its stories created.
            - **Tooltip:** the "Drafted" tooltip says how many stories were added to the Backlog.

            While editing I also fixed an existing wrapping problem: a line beginning "says:" had been compiled as a stray "Says" heading in that section.
            \\\

    sentFrom: DraftingADraftCardCreatesItsStoriesAnd

    model: opus
